 |created by=H. P. Lovecraft
 }}
: The Whisperer in Darkness)}}

B-67 is a fictional character created by H. P. Lovecraft for his horror sci-fi novella The Whisperer in Darkness.

### Overview
B-67 is the identification assigned to a man who met the Mi-Go in the Himalayas and became one of their agents on Earth, helping the alien creatures in various ways. In return, the Mi-Go gave him the opportunity to travel through the universe.

As of 1928, B-67 had traveled to 37 different celestial bodies, including "planets, dark stars, and less definable objects". Of these, eight were outside the Milky Way Galaxy, and two outside of "the curved cosmos of space and time" itself. Each time, the trip was accomplished non-corporeally, as the technology of the Mi-Go allows them to casually and harmlessly remove the brains of humans and other species and store them in brain cylinders for easy transportation, later plugging the cylinders to other devices that enable the brains to experience sight, sound, and speech. In the meantime, the brainless bodies of B-67 and the others remain preserved in stasis, without aging, stored in underground places such as the interior of Vermont's Round Hill, waiting for the travelers' return.

When Albert Wilmarth was invited to Henry Akeley's house by a creature masquerading as Akeley, he was shown the Mi-Go brain cylinders, including one containing the brain of B-67, which was plugged to the speech-machine. B-67 told Wilmarth that he was familiar with him by sight and reputation, and had kept close track of the correspondence between Wilmarth and Akeley. On behalf of the Mi-Go, he invited Wilmarth to join him in their next trip to Yuggoth, claiming that Akeley and Noyes would be accompanying them as well. He assured Wilmarth that the procedure was entirely safe and painless, and that life as a disembodied brain was pleasant, for when the cylinder is disconnected, "one merely drops off into a sleep of especially vivid and fantastic dreams". (: The Whisperer in Darkness)

