B'gnu-Thun (The Soul-Chilling Ice-God) is a Great Old One from the expanded Cthulhu Mythos. The character is only briefly mentioned in the James Ambuehl short stories "The Stalker in the Snows" and "Correlated Contents", in which B'gnu-Thun is described as being the twin brother of Ruhtra Dyoll, and appears as a humanoid figure made of ice followed by an eerie blizzard.

: "The Inhabitant of the Lake"
|created by=Ramsey Campbell
}}

Baalblo is a fictional celestial body created by British author Ramsey Campbell for his 1964 short story "The Inhabitant of the Lake."

### Overview
Baalblo is a dead star which forms part of a binary system with Yifne, its green-hued twin. It is mentioned in passing in The Revelations of Glaaki, and is orbited by Tond, home of the intelligent humanoids known as yarkdao. It is likely that the Great Old One Glaaki passed by Baalblo before making its way to Earth.

: "The Moon-Bog" 
|appearances = 
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft
}}

Ballylough is a fictional village in County Meath, Ireland. It is located near the village of Kilderry and is mentioned in the story "The Moon-Bog" by H. P. Lovecraft. Ballylough was probably based on the real world Ballinlough, also in Meath.

: "The White Ship"
|appearances = : The Complete Dreamlands
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft
}}


Basil Elton is the narrator and protagonist of H. P. Lovecraft's Dunsanian story "The White Ship". He is the keeper of the North Point lighthouse, and once a regular visitor to the Dreamlands. His visits to the Dreamlands ended when he was killed in a shipwreck there, leaving him stranded in the Waking World. (: "The White Ship")

The Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, in which Elton appears as a non-player character, locates the North Point in Kingsport, Massachusetts. (: The Complete Dreamlands) This is likely a reference to The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath, in which Randolph Carter mentions "a lighthouse-keeper in ancient Kingsport" heavily implied to be Basil Elton.

: "The Call of Cthulhu"
}}


The Bat-Winged Devils are a type of fictional creature briefly made mention of in H. P. Lovecraft's 1928 short story "The Call of Cthulhu".

### Overview
Little is revealed of these creatures, other than that they were a species of winged beasts, possibly subterranean in nature, which inhabited the Louisiana bayous south of New Orleans, and seemed to worship the white polypous thing also mentioned in "The Call of Cthulhu".

: CthulhuTech
}}

The Blood Brigade is a fictional organization in the CthulhuTech pencil-and-paper roleplaying game by Wildfire LLC.

### Background
The Blood Brigade is an organization of fanatical religious zealots organized into terrorist cells which was founded by a wealthy former television evangelist. Who are waging a war of terror against the godless, the unclean, and Nazzadi. The Death Shadows supply them with explosives and weapons, even though the Blood Brigade is waging war against the Mythos. As their bigotry and hatred results in violent crime and acts of terror against innocents or which results in casualties among innocents. It's lunatic members spread like the plague, literally society's cockroaches. They teach that man is being punished by God, who sent the horrors of the Aeon War upon mankind. It recruits its members from the disillusioned and depressed. Initiation usually involves a violent crime and ends in a bloody ritual.

: Secrets of Japan
}}


The Bodhisattva are part of the Buddhist cosmology, and were first used by Chaosium in 2005 for the Call of Cthulhu role-playing game.

### Background
The Bodhisattva are entities who have refrained from reaching full enlightenment and therefore ascending to Nirvana, so they can assist other mortals in becoming enlightened. They are the messengers of Lord Buddha, carrying out his will. Each of them has its own particular promise or concern on which it focuses its energies. They have a 99% understanding of how the universe works, and therefore have great power.

Bodhisattva manifest in consecrated temples to those whom have fasted or meditated for hours, and appear as shining beings of golden plasma energy who usually tower over mortals. Depending on the natural inclination of the supplicant is the gender it takes, and usually as they are rendered in paintings or statues. In its manifestation, supplicants see their own truth, therefore they may confuse the being with something from their belief system (e.g. angels).

: "Ubbo-Sathla"
|created by = Clark Ashton Smith
}}


The Brood of Ubbo-Sathla are a fictional race created by Clark Ashton Smith for his 1933 short story "Ubbo-Sathla". They were later incorporated into Chaosium's role-playing game Call of Cthulhu. (: Malleus Monstrorum)

### Background
The Brood of Ubbo-Sathla are the Outer God's primal spawn which look like giant one-celled creatures, no two of which are alike. They are constantly produced by Ubbo-Sathla's formless mass. Some of them are devoured by their mindless sire, and those that escape fulfill its every urge and desire. These creatures are usually found in its lair and will ambush their targets. Using their gelatinous tentacles, sticky filaments, etc to draw the victim into their body where it's digested.

: Secrets of Japan
}}

The Brotherhood of the Black Lotus was founded in 6th century BC. They are caretakers of the Forbidden Sutra and its learned masters have a lineage that goes all the way back to the Emerald Lama (an avatar of Hastur). Its headquarters is believed to be either in China or near China, at the nearly-mythical Shamballa within the Himalayan or Kunlun mountains of Tibet or the Altai mountains of Mongolia. Secrecy and deception is the stock and trade of the Brotherhood, with its countless minions, false fronts, and layers of dead ends. The organization has influence over most of man's esoteric religons and because of its role in the Asian drug trade, it's a wealthy organization.

For centuries it has played a major role in the Asian drug trade. It's believed that the real leaders of the Golden Triangle drug operations (and the Chinese Triads) are members of the Brotherhood. Members of the organization practice Buddhist tantric rituals. Known as the Green Caps they have waged a holy war with the mainstream sects of Buddhism (the Yellow Caps) as long as they have existed. Their goal is to bring about the age of corruption and the end of mankind's dominance on Earth with the return of the Great Old Ones. They trade with the Tcho-Tcho on a regular basis and have strong ties to the plateaus of Sung and Leng.

