Camorin is a region in the Dreamlands briefly mentioned in "The White Ship" by H. P. Lovecraft. When Basil Elton spoke of the wonders of the fabled land of Cathuria, he compared its forests of aloe and sandalwood to the "fragrant groves" of Camorin.

: Masks of Nyarlathotep
|created_by=Larry DiTillioLynn Willis
}}

The Carlyle Expedition was a scientific research venture which journeyed to Egypt. It was led by an archaeologist named Roger Carlyle, and it is from him that the group took their name. The expedition eventually went missing (: Masks of Nyarlathotep).

: "Polaris" 
}}


Cassiopeia is a real-world constellation in the northern sky, named after the Greek mythological queen Cassiopeia, the mother of Andromeda.

### In the Mythos
Cassiopeia is briefly mentioned in the short story "Polaris" by H. P. Lovecraft. The constellation is formed by five bright stars in the shape of a letter W.

: H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands
 }}

Cats from Uranus are a fictional species created by Chaosium for the role-playing game Call of Cthulhu.

### Description
A sapient species native to the Dreamlands of Uranus, these six-legged felines stand neutral in the wars between the cats of Earth and their Saturnian brethren. Like the other two species, the Cats from Uranus can transport themselves between worlds by jumping. Their physical form is more "substantial", compared to the Cats from Saturn, with rough leathery orange skin patterned with reddish-brown spots.

The Cats from Uranus can also be identified by their long whip-like tail armed with a spine at the tip, and by three pairs of horn-like sensorial appendages on the sides of their heads, which can detect heat and other sources of energy at a distance of up to 40 km. (: H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands)

: "The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog"
|created by = Glenn Rahman and Richard L. Tierney
}}

The Cats of Sadoqua are a fictional species of black supernatural felines created by Glenn Rahman and Richard L. Tierney for their short story "The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog", in which they're depicted as servants of Sadoqua (i.e. Tsathoggua).

### History
In the first century AD, the inhabitants of the Gaulish province of Averonia summoned the Cats of Sadoqua to battle against their enemies, the Romans, and the Druid worshippers of Sheila-Na-Gog. (: "The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog")

: "Beyond the Wall of Sleep"  
}}


The Catskill Mountains, also known as the Catskills, are a range of mountains in southeastern New York State, and are a subrange of the larger Appalachian Mountains. 

### Lovecraft's Fiction 
Joe Slater lived in a backwoods community of generational hill dwellers in the Catskills before he was confined to a mental hospital for his killing of Peter Slader. (: "Beyond the Wall of Sleep")

: Thor Vol #130 
|created by = Stan Lee, Jack Kirby
}}


The Cerberus is a monster of Greek mythology, which fought Hercules. The Marvel adaptation was created in 1966 by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby and made its first appearance in issue #130 of the 1st volume of the Thor comic book series.

### Background
The Cerberus is a descendant of the Elder God Set. It was sired by Set's granddaughter Echidna who mated with Typhon, one of the Titans. A multi-headed large dog with a scorpion tail which guards the gates of Tartarus. It can shapeshift into a larger dog or into a minotaur-like humanoid. When in humanoid form it's able to transmute objects into weapons, carries a warhammer, and fires blasts from his helmet.

: "Herbert West--Reanimator" 
|appearances = 
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft
}}

The Chapman house was a deserted farmhouse on the outskirts of the fictional town of Arkham, Massachusetts, from the story "Herbert West--Reanimator" by H. P. Lovecraft. The house was located beyond Meadow Hill in an isolated position away from roads and other houses. For this reason Herbert West and his friend, the unnamed narrator of the story, used the house as a clandestine laboratory for their experiments involving the reanimation of the dead. 

When the corpse of one of their human test subjects was reanimated in the laboratory, it began screaming and fled the house. In their ensuing panic, West and the narrator likewise fled but knocked over an acetylene lamp while doing so, causing the house to go up in flames. They escaped the fire but the house was completely destroyed. (: "Herbert West--Reanimator")

: "Polaris" 
}}


Charles' Wain (or Charles' Wagon) is an older name for the Big Dipper, a group (asterism) of seven bright stars in the constellation Ursa Major in the northern sky. Charles' Wain is mentioned in the story "Polaris" by H. P. Lovecraft, where the unnamed narrator observes the Wagon as it "lumbers up from behind the vapour-soaked swamp trees that sway in the night wind."

Polaris, the current northern pole star, can be located by extending an imaginary straight line through Merak and Dubhe, the two stars in the front of the bowl of the Big Dipper, making it useful for celestial navigation.

: "The Inhabitant of the Lake"
|created by=Ramsey Campbell
}}

Chig is an entity first made mention of in The Revelations of Glaaki, which appears to have been its only reference to date (: "The Inhabitant of the Lake"). It is apparently some form of deity worshipped by the alien yarkdao, whose Priests of Chig undertake devotional rituals lasting three-and-quarter years in length.

: "Black Man with a Horn"
|created by = T. E. D. Klein
}}

The Children of Shugoran are a fictional race created by American author T. E. D. Klein for his short story "Black Man with a Horn", and later expanded upon by Chaosium for their Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game.

### Background
The Children of Shugoran are smaller, less physically-powerful copies of Shugoran, an avatar of Nyarlathotep. Those followers of Nyarlathotep who have performed particularly well may be given a Child of Shugoran as a familiar of sorts.

: "Imprisoned with the Pharaohs"
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft and Harry Houdini
}}
: "Imprisoned with the Pharaohs")}}


The Children of the Sphinx are fictional beings created by H. P. Lovecraft and Harry Houdini for their horror short story "Imprisoned with the Pharaohs". Their name originates from Chaosium's Call of Cthulhu RPG.

### Background
The Children of the Sphinx are a race of monstrous humanoids who reside in Egypt and the surrounding deserts. They come in a variety of forms, human-animal composites with an animal head on a human body.
In "Imprisoned with the Pharaohs", it is suggested that they are the restless result of mummies being constructed from mixed human and animal parts. They worship the Sphinx deep beneath the Earth, presided over by [https://lovecraft.fandom.com/wiki/Khephren Khephren] and [https://lovecraft.fandom.com/wiki/Nitocris Nitocris].

