The Children of the Wind are a fictional species created by Joseph Payne Brennan for his horror short story "City of the Seven Winds". They were incorporated into the Cthulhu Mythos by the role-playing game supplement Malleus Monstrorum.

### Description
The Children of the Wind are grotesque carnivorous creatures that float high in the air above the mythical City of the Seven Winds, somewhere in the Syrian desert. Their appearance has been compared to "human abortions" imbued with "half-liquid life", as well as "monstrous elementals" speculated to originate from "some infinitely distant end of the universe". They have white eyes, grey mouths, and stubby appendages shaped like "slimy stumps".

Members of this species are predators which feast on human flesh. They hunt in large groups and playfully mutilate the still-living prey before eating it. While they've been described as elementals, it's not known whether these creatures are responsible for the violent winds that perpetually assail the City of the Seven Winds. The sorcerers who make up the entire population of the city keep it usually invisible, but lift the invisibility spell occasionally to attract travelers. These unfortunate visitors are brought to a high tower where a window will be opened, allowing them to be consumed by the Children of the Wind and thus preventing the monsters from foraging on the streets below. (: "City of the Seven Winds")

: Ye Booke of Monstres II
|created by = Scott David Aniolowski
}}

The Children of Y'golonac are a fictional race created by Scott David Aniolowski for the Call of Cthulhu gaming supplement Ye Booke of Monstres II. They are based on Y'golonac, a Great Old One who first appeared in Ramsey Campbell's short story "Cold Print".

### Background
The Children of Y'golonac are a race of small humanoids whom are eyeless and deformed. Like Y'golonac, their palms have biting fanged mouths. Because of their blindness, they possess a heightened sense of smell and hearing. The nearly mindless creatures swarm their victims in packs.

: "The Curse of Yig"
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft and Zealia Bishop
}}


The Children of Yig is the name for snakes that are sacred to Yig. 

### Description
The Children of Yig can be any variety of snake, but all have a white marking on their foreheads denoting their status. Venom from a Child of Yig cannot be cured with any known methods, and disturbing the Children of Yig could transform one into a Spawn of Yig. (: Malleus Monstrorum)

: "House of Strangers"
|created_by = Gail Simone & Dale Eaglesham
}}

The Children of Arion is a fictional organisation created by DC Comics for their Secret Six comic series.

### Background
The Children of Arion are a cult dedicated to the Elder Gods. The members appear to be normal humans but this is just an illusion, as they are actually mutants with reptilian or fish-like features. They claim to be True Atlanteans and later manipulated the Secret Six, a group of super-villains and sometimes heroes, to help bring about the return of the Elder Gods. The cult came to an end after failing their mission, with the members being consumed by Thrumm. (: "House of Strangers")

