Christchurch Cemetery is a cemetery in the fictional town of Arkham, Massachusetts. At one point Herbert West considered using the cemetery as a source of bodies for his reanimation experiments, but decided that the embalmed state of those buried there would not have been suitable for this purpose. Later, the reanimated corpse of Dr. Allan Halsey killed a night watchman at the cemetery when the corpse attempted to return to its tomb. (: "Herbert West--Reanimator")

: The Bermuda Triangle
 }}


The Chupacabras is a mysterious creature reported from folklore, cryptozoology and popular culture. Its name is Spanish for "goat-sucker" and refers to its alleged habit of killing animals and draining them of their blood. Originating from Puerto Rico, the Chupacabras legend has spread to several areas of North, Central and South America, and even to other continents.

In the Cthulhu Mythos, the creature is described and illustrated in the role-playing game supplement The Bermuda Triangle. The game portrays it as a reptilian semi-humanoid monster reminiscent of the original legend, as opposed to the quadrupedal mangy canine image that has become popular in the United States.

### In the Mythos
The Chupacabras is a mysterious predator infamous for attacking small animals such as goats, dogs and chickens. It feeds by sucking the blood from its prey. Found exclusively in the countryside, it is never sighted in cities, and doesn't seem to attack humans. Originally reported from Puerto Rico, it has since been sighted in Florida as well, suggesting that there might be an entire species of such creatures, not just a single aberration. (: The Bermuda Triangle)

: CthulhuTech
|title1=The Circle
}}

The Circle is a fictional organisation in the CthulhuTech pencil-and-paper role-playing game by Wildfire LLC.

### Background
The Circle is a cult that is dispersed throughout the world, small numbers existing in every arcology. The organization maintains a maintains a tight network. It's members serve none other than Nyarlathotep. An organization consisting of para-psychics and dark sorcerers. They are independent of the Chrysalis Corporation, operating as the elite special forces of the Children of Chaos. Nyarlathotep has given them the task of gathering the world's mystical powers into their hands. It's members operate as assassins and spies for the Chrysalis Corporation. They undermine and attack the government agencies which oppose them. It also is deeply involved in the black market in illegal magic trade, and doing business with them is dangerous as they are not trustworthy.

: End of the World (scenario "Thunder in the Blood")
|created by   = Kevin A. Ross
}}

The Cloud-Thing is a fictional entity created by American author and games designer Kevin A. Ross for the role-playing game Call of Cthulhu. It first appeared in "Thunder in the Blood", a scenario included in the supplement End of the World (Triad Entertainments, 1994).

### Overview
The Cloud-Thing is one of five Lesser Other Gods from the Court of Azathoth that appeared during a summoning of Azathoth near San Lucas. It appears as a cloudy mass with shimmering bursts of purplish light emanating from within it, as well as a funnel-like extension that reaches almost to the ground beneath, which it uses to suck victims into itself. It leaves behind a light rain of purplish crystals in its wake. (: End of the World)

: "Polaris" 
}}


Coma Berenices is a constellation in the northern sky, translated from the Latin as "Berenice's Hair". It was named after Queen Berenice II of Egypt who sacrificed her long hair as a votive offering. Coma Berenices is mentioned in the short story "Polaris" by H. P. Lovecraft, where the unnamed narrator observes the constellation as it "shimmers weirdly far off in the mysterious east."

: "Herbert West--Reanimator" 
|created by = H. P. Lovecraft
}}

The Commercial House is a fictional restaurant or hotel created by the late American author Howard Phillips Lovecraft for his serial horror story "Herbert West--Reanimator."

### Overview
Located in the fictional town of Arkham, Massachusetts, the Commercial House has a bar where drinks are served. Herbert West and his friend, the unnamed narrator of the story, were drinking there after the funeral of Dr. Allan Halsey. They decided that night to steal his body from Christchurch Cemetery so they could attempt to reanimate it.

: "The Alchemist"
|created by=H. P. Lovecraft
}}

Comte Louis de C-- (no surname given) was a French count in the story "The Alchemist" by H. P. Lovecraft. He was the son of Comte Robert and an ancestor of Comte Antoine. Like other members of his line, Louis was killed at the age of 32, a victim of the curse put on his family by Charles le Sorcier who drowned Louis in the castle moat.

### Behind the Mythos
In the comic book adaptation from The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft series, the C-- stands for Chabrillane.

: "The Alchemist"
|created by=H. P. Lovecraft
}}

Comte Robert de C-- (no surname given) was a French count in the story "The Alchemist" by H. P. Lovecraft. He was the son of Comte Godfrey, the father of Louis, and an ancestor of Comte Antoine. Like other members of his line, Robert was killed at the age of 32, a victim of the curse put on his family by Charles le Sorcier. He had been out walking in a field and was confronted by Charles who forced lethal poison down Robert's throat, leaving him there to die.

### Behind the Mythos
In the comic book adaptation from The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft series, the C-- stands for Chabrillane.

: "Winged Death"
 |created by=Hazel Heald and H. P. Lovecraft
 }}

Dr. Cornelius Van Keulen is a fictional character from the horror short story "Winged Death", by H. P. Lovecraft and Hazel Heald.

### Overview
Dr. Cornelius Van Keulen was a physician who worked with coroner Johannes Bogaert in Bloemfontein, South Africa. On January 24, 1932, the two were called when a man was found dead in his room in the Orange Hotel. Bogaert and Dr. Van Keulen inspected the scene, along with constable Ian De Witt, and the hotel's manager George C. Titteridge. It was Van Keulen who recognised the dead man as a fellow doctor, Thomas Slauenwite, and read aloud the contents of his notebook. All four reacted with shock and horror to the events narrated and the significance of certain words written in black ink on the high ceiling that no man could possibly reach, as there was no furniture inside the room to allow it. (: "Winged Death")

: "Count Magnus"
 |created by=M. R. James
 }}
: "Count Magnus")}}


Count Magnus' companion is a fictional monster created by M. R. James for his horror short story "Count Magnus". It was incorporated into the Cthulhu Mythos by Brian Lumley in "The House of the Temple".

### Description
When the 17th century Swedish nobleman, alchemist, and cruel sorcerer Magnus de la Gardie made a "Black Pilgrimage" to the cursed village of Chorazin, he brought back a "faithful messenger" with him. From a distance, this mysterious companion appears like a short humanoid wearing a hooded garment that hides its body and face. A closer look, however, reveals that the creature is not human, but possesses tentacles like those of a "devil-fish". It kills humans by sucking out the flesh from their faces. The unfortunate victims are found dead with a bare skull where the face used to be.

While the origins of the Count's companion are unknown, it appears to be associated with the "prince of the air", an entity that the Count saluted in Chorazin to obtain the secrets of immortal life and his "faithful messenger". Like the Count himself, the companion appears to be immortal. (: "Count Magnus")

Gavin McGilchrist found evidence that the Great Old Ones have "familiars", which are actually parasitic creatures that "live on Them as lice live on men". When mortals attempt to contact the Great Old Ones, they might end up with one of these beings instead. McGilchrist believed that "the sucking, hunting thing that Count Magnus kept" was one of these familiars. (: "The House of the Temple")

