Ian De Witt was a police constable of the Central Station in Bloemfontein, South Africa. On January 24, 1932, when Thomas Slauenwite was found dead in his room in the Orange Hotel, De Witt was one of the four men who inspected the scene, along with the hotel's manager George C. Titteridge, coroner Johannes Bogaert, and coroner's physician Dr. Cornelius Van Keulen. It was De Witt who glanced upwards and noticed a series of words written in black ink on the white ceiling, even though the room had no furniture that could allow a person to reach up and write them. When Dr. Van Keulen read aloud the contents of the dead man's notebook, all four reacted with shock and horror to the events narrated and the significance of the words on the ceiling. (: "Winged Death")

