Kallikantzaroi (singular: Kallikantzaros) are a legendary species of goblin-like creatures from Southeast European folklore who spend most of their time underground, but emerge to the surface around Christmastime to cause mischief.

In The Whisperer in Darkness, by H. P. Lovecraft, the narrator Albert Wilmarth mentions "the kallikanzari of modern Greece" as one of several examples of folkloric creatures that he deems comparable to the alien crustaceans which have been sighted in Vermont. Other examples include the satyrs, fauns and dryads of the ancient world, the "hidden races of troglodytes and burrowers" from Wales and Ireland, and the "Abominable Snow-Men" of the Himalayas.

Kallikantzaroi were later featured in the role-playing game supplement Cthulhu Invictus, although the word is spelled as "kalikantzaris" (singular and plural). The game classifies them as a lesser independent race.

