The Testaments of Carnamagos is a fictional grimoire created by Clark Ashton Smith for his horror story "The Treader of the Dust". It was incorporated into the Cthulhu Mythos by the role-playing game Call of Cthulhu under the slightly altered title Testament of Carnamagos.

### Background
This exceedingly rare tome was written in ancient times by an "evil sage and seer" named Carnamagos. Only two copies are known to have ever existed, the original having been recovered from a Graeco-Bactrian tomb and transcribed in the original Greek by an apostate monk in the Middle Ages, using the blood of "an incubus-begotten monster" as ink. One of the copies was destroyed by the Spanish Inquisition in the early 13th century, while the other, bound in covers of shagreen with hasps made of human bone, found its way to a book dealer who sold it to occultist John Sebastian. Previously, Sebastian had believed that the book was "a mere medieval legend". (: "The Treader of the Dust")

### Contents
Carnamagos' book contains "the chronicles of great sorcerers of old, and the histories of demons earthly and ultra-cosmic, and the veritable spells by which the demons could be called up and controlled and dismissed". It includes information about Quachil Uttaus, an obscure entity from beyond time and space which causes its surroundings to age and decay in instants. The incantation to summon this being is preceded by a warning, which cautions that even reading the spell silently might, if the reader has any conscious or subconscious death wish, summon Quachil Uttaus to destroy them. (: "The Treader of the Dust")

