The Whisperer in Darkness is a fictional entity featured in H. P. Lovecraft's eponymous novella. Named for its whispering voice and preference for a dark environment, this non-human creature wore a human face and hands (either genuine or replicas) in order to disguise itself and impersonate Henry Wentworth Akeley. Clues in the story suggest that this entity was either a Mi-Go or one of their gods, namely Nyarlathotep, based on the Mi-Go's ritual prophesying that Nyarlathotep "shall put on the semblance of men, the waxen mask and the robe that hides, and come down from the world of Seven Suns to mock".

### In Lovecraft Circle Works
In 1928, Henry Akeley started corresponding with Miskatonic University professor Albert Wilmarth, telling him about his encounters with the extraterrestrial beings known as the Outer Ones, or Mi-Go. Having already made a phonograph record and stolen the Mi-Go's black stone to study its hieroglyphs, Akeley soon became a target of these creatures and their human agents, who didn't want people intruding into their business or exposing them to the world. When the conflict escalated, culminating in direct attacks against Akeley's farm, Akeley was eventually captured and a forged letter was sent in his name to Wilmarth, telling him that the hostilities had ceased and inviting him to visit Akeley in his farm, so the latter could explain things. Wilmarth was picked up at the train station by a man named Noyes, who informed him that Akeley was ill, but was still eager to talk to him.

Wilmarth had seen a photograph of the real Akeley, and had no doubts about his host's identity, as the mask worn by the impersonator was either Akeley's actual face or a perfect replica. Meanwhile, the alleged illness provided a plausible explanation for the whisperer's pale inexpressive visage and abnormal voice. However, although it spoke only in whispers, the voice was still strong enough to be heard clearly from across the room.

This false Akeley was clad in a loose dressing-gown, his feet and ankles bandaged (which he claimed was because they were swollen due to the disease), and had a vivid yellow scarf wrapped around his head and neck. The being stood in the dark, claiming that the illness made his eyes sensitive. It moved only rarely and refused to eat or drink anything in Wilmarth's presence. Akeley's bushy mustache, along with the dim illumination, combined to make any movement of the being's mouth imperceptible. Wilmarth did notice that the room had a "queer odor" and a "sense of vibration", which he later realised must have been attributes of the Whisperer itself, as they were no longer felt when the creature was absent.

The Whisperer told Wilmarth about the Mi-Go's science and history, the nature of the universe and other dimensions, and about beings like Tsathoggua, Cthulhu, the Doels, the Hounds of Tindalos, Yig, and Azathoth. At last, it

