Sir William Brinton is a fictional character created by H. P. Lovecraft for his horror short story "The Rats in the Walls".

### Biography
Sir William Brinton was a renowned British archaeologist who took part in the 1923 expedition to explore the ancient catacombs beneath Exham Priory, along with Delapore, Edward Norrys, Dr. Trask, Thornton, and two other scholars. Brinton used a counterweight to lift the heavy altar door, allowing the group to descend into an underground passage which, according to his observations, had been chiseled upwards from beneath. The passage was covered in human and semi-human skeletal remains, and eventually led into a massive grotto containing both bones and ruins. While the rest of the party gasped in shock and Thornton even fainted, Sir William was the only man who maintained his composure in the face of this bizarre and unexpected discovery. Later, he translated the writings in the ruins, which revealed that the degenerated humans were raised as livestock by the cannibal members of certain cults which persisted for several centuries. (: "The Rats in the Walls")

Witchcraft is a practice of magic that encompasses many different types of activities including astrology, divination, spell casting, and spirit communication. It includes the practices of many cultures, nations and religions as well as many books and writings from ancient times. 

The practice of witchcraft infuses its practitioners, known as witches, with remarkable paranormal abilities such as move objects without touching them (telekinesis), kill at a distance via strange rituals etc. In popular culture, witchcraft is the supposed art of black magic associated with the Devil or it has also seen many religions related to it such as Voodoo (Vodun) or Wicca. Practitioners of Witchcraft have been persecuted and executed for centuries by the Christian Church.

Witches in the Cthulhu Mythos are distinguished as being in general, and no matter what the underlying fictional "reality" of their witchcraft might actually prove to be:

* female;
* allied with or directly serving an Old One;
* possessed of far more than normal lore in relation to the Mythos;
* contact with a Dark Man or Black Man entity or other ruler of a traditional witches' coven;
* falling foul of the forces of conventional religion, morality and their local community.

Keziah Mason is an excellent example of this sort of Mythos character.

The reference above to the underlying "reality" of witchcraft is due to the fact that the earliest Lovecraft references to witchcraft still seem somewhat supernatural, whereas by the time of his writing "The Dreams in the Witch-House" he has very definitely moved to the position that witchcraft and all "magic" is a higher science or a hidden form of mathematics, allowing the use of esoteric physics to manipulate physical reality and bend time and space.

This conceptualization removes any pact with the Devil or similar supernatural element with the exception of being contacted by or possibly corrupted by the Dark Man entity, often treated as cognate with Nyarlathotep, who presumably introduces the secret mathematical knowledge to those unable to access it by the decoding of the concealed information in grimoires, which in this version of witchcraft would be more like physics textbooks albeit ones using peculiar symbology to disguise information not meant for the uninitiated.

Witchcraft stands in contrast to the familiar, sane and orderly world from which protagonists in Mythos stories often depart. It seems at first glance to be in the vanguard of disorder, chaos and disruption and this is broadly true of conventional old fashioned attitudes to black magic and "witchcraft" in general: a perversion or inversion of a perceived natural order. However allied with H.P. Lovecraft's cosmicism is the view that since humanity is of no central importance neither is any human concept of order or morality. Thus the matured Lovecraftian view of magic-as-unknown-science, a concept of magic use as mathematical formulae stripped of any moral axis. This in some re

